Experiment 8 — Hadoop MapReduce Word Count
1. Update Ubuntu and install Java
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y openjdk-11-jdk openssh-client openssh-server rsync wget nano
java -version
javac -version


2. Configure SSH
sudo systemctl enable --now ssh
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
ssh-keygen -t rsa -P '' -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
ssh localhost


If asked to trust the host, type yes. Then run exit.
3. Download and install Hadoop 3.3.6
cd ~
wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/hadoop/common/hadoop-3.3.6/hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz
tar -xzf hadoop-3.3.6.tar.gz
sudo mv hadoop-3.3.6 /usr/local/hadoop
sudo chown -R "$USER":"$USER" /usr/local/hadoop


4. Set environment variables
nano ~/.bashrc


Paste at the bottom:
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64
export HADOOP_HOME=/usr/local/hadoop
export HADOOP_INSTALL=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_MAPRED_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_COMMON_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_HDFS_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export YARN_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_CONF_DIR=$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop
export PATH=$PATH:$HADOOP_HOME/bin:$HADOOP_HOME/sbin


Save with Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X.
source ~/.bashrc
hadoop version


5. Configure Hadoop Java
nano "$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/hadoop-env.sh"


Set this line:
export JAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/java-11-openjdk-amd64


Save and exit.
6. Configure core-site.xml
nano "$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/core-site.xml"


Replace its contents:
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>fs.defaultFS</name>
        <value>hdfs://localhost:9000</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


7. Configure hdfs-site.xml
nano "$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/hdfs-site.xml"


Replace its contents:
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.replication</name>
        <value>1</value>
    </property>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.namenode.name.dir</name>
        <value>file:///usr/local/hadoop/data/hdfs/namenode</value>
    </property>
    <property>
        <name>dfs.datanode.data.dir</name>
        <value>file:///usr/local/hadoop/data/hdfs/datanode</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


8. Configure mapred-site.xml
nano "$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/mapred-site.xml"


Replace its contents:
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>mapreduce.framework.name</name>
        <value>yarn</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


9. Configure yarn-site.xml
nano "$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/yarn-site.xml"


Replace its contents:
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<configuration>
    <property>
        <name>yarn.nodemanager.aux-services</name>
        <value>mapreduce_shuffle</value>
    </property>
</configuration>


Save each XML file using Ctrl+O, Enter, and Ctrl+X.
10. Format HDFS
mkdir -p /usr/local/hadoop/data/hdfs/namenode
mkdir -p /usr/local/hadoop/data/hdfs/datanode

# Run only for a fresh installation
hdfs namenode -format


Warning: Do not format again after storing data in HDFS.
11. Start Hadoop services
start-dfs.sh
jps

start-yarn.sh
jps


Expected processes include NameNode, DataNode, SecondaryNameNode, ResourceManager, and NodeManager.
12. Create the input file
mkdir -p ~/hadoop-lab
cd ~/hadoop-lab

cat > file.txt <<'EOF'
Hello Hadoop
Hello World
Hadoop is powerful
EOF

cat file.txt


13. Upload the file to HDFS
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /input
hdfs dfs -put -f file.txt /input/
hdfs dfs -ls /input
hdfs dfs -cat /input/file.txt


14. Execute MapReduce Word Count
# Remove old output if it exists
hdfs dfs -rm -r -f /output

# Run Word Count
hadoop jar "$HADOOP_HOME/share/hadoop/mapreduce/hadoop-mapreduce-examples-3.3.6.jar" wordcount /input /output


15. Display the output
hdfs dfs -cat /output/part-r-00000


16. Stop Hadoop
stop-yarn.sh
stop-dfs.sh
jps



Experiment 9 — Apache Spark
This section uses Spark 3.5.8 and Java 11.
1. Install Python
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip
python3 --version
java -version


2. Download and install Spark
cd ~
wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/spark/spark-3.5.8/spark-3.5.8-bin-hadoop3.tgz
tar -xzf spark-3.5.8-bin-hadoop3.tgz
sudo mv spark-3.5.8-bin-hadoop3 /usr/local/spark
sudo chown -R "$USER":"$USER" /usr/local/spark


3. Configure Spark environment variables
nano ~/.bashrc


Add these lines at the bottom:
export SPARK_HOME=/usr/local/spark
export PATH=$PATH:$SPARK_HOME/bin


Save and exit.
source ~/.bashrc
echo "$SPARK_HOME"
spark-shell --version
pyspark --version


4. Start Scala Spark shell
spark-shell --master 'local[2]'


Enter the following commands inside the scala> prompt, one at a time.
5. Create a sample text file
import java.nio.file.{Files, Paths}

Files.write(
  Paths.get(System.getProperty("user.home") + "/input.txt"),
  "Hello Spark\nHello Hadoop\nSpark is powerful".getBytes("UTF-8")
)


6. Read the file and count lines
val lines = sc.textFile("file://" + System.getProperty("user.home") + "/input.txt")
lines.count()


Expected result: 3.
7. Word Count using RDD
val words = lines.flatMap(line => line.split("\\s+"))
val counts = words.map(word => (word, 1)).reduceByKey(_ + _)
counts.collect().foreach(println)


8. Exit Scala Spark shell
:quit


9. Start PySpark
Run in the normal Ubuntu terminal:
pyspark --master 'local[2]'


Enter these commands inside the >>> prompt.
10. Word Count using Python
rdd = sc.parallelize([    "Hello Spark",    "Hello Hadoop",    "Spark is powerful"])counts = (    rdd.flatMap(lambda line: line.split())       .map(lambda word: (word, 1))       .reduceByKey(lambda a, b: a + b))print(sorted(counts.collect()))



Exit PySpark:
exit()



11. Create a CSV file
Run in the normal Ubuntu terminal:
cat > ~/students.csv <<'EOF'
id,name,age
1,Alice,22
2,Bob,25
EOF


12. Load CSV into a DataFrame
Start PySpark:
pyspark --master 'local[2]'


At the >>> prompt, enter:
import osdf = spark.read.option("header", True).option(    "inferSchema", True).csv("file://" + os.path.expanduser("~/students.csv"))df.show()



13. Display DataFrame schema
df.printSchema()df.select("name", "age").show()



Exit PySpark:
exit()



14. Optional: Read Hadoop HDFS data using Spark
First, start HDFS in the normal terminal:
start-dfs.sh
hdfs dfs -ls /input


Start PySpark:
pyspark --master 'local[2]'


At the >>> prompt:
lines = sc.textFile("hdfs://localhost:9000/input/file.txt")print(lines.count())counts = (    lines.flatMap(lambda line: line.split())         .map(lambda word: (word, 1))         .reduceByKey(lambda a, b: a + b))print(sorted(counts.collect()))



Exit PySpark and stop Hadoop:
stop-yarn.sh
stop-dfs.sh


Remember: XML code must go inside its corresponding configuration file. Scala code belongs inside scala>, Python code inside >>>, and shell commands in the normal Ubuntu terminal.